  "title": "Dependency pinning: the lockfile lie",
  "content": "The obvious answer to a software composition question in code review is usually the incomplete one. It only shows up under the exact conditions nobody tests for. This is the version of the write-up that includes the part that broke.\n\nMost teams believe a lockfile guarantees reproducible builds. It does not. A lockfile pins the exact versions of transitive dependencies that were resolved at the time it was generated. But the resolution itself depends on the registry state, the package manager version, the platform, and the order in which dependencies are declared. Change any of those, and the same lockfile can produce a different dependency graph. The result is that two builds from the same commit can differ in ways that are invisible until something breaks at 2 a.m.\n\nSoftware composition analysis (SCA) tools are supposed to catch this. They scan your manifest and lockfile, compare against vulnerability databases, and flag risky packages. But SCA and pinning are often treated as separate concerns: SCA for compliance, pinning for stability. In practice, they are two sides of the same coin. A pinning strategy that ignores SCA gives you false confidence; an SCA process that ignores pinning gives you a flood of alerts you cannot act on. This post is about closing that gap: how to design a dependency management workflow that is both reproducible and secure, without drowning in noise. The real problem is that most teams have no explicit policy for when to pin, what to pin, and how to respond to the alerts that SCA generates.\n\n## The lockfile is not a reproducibility guarantee\n\nThe lockfile's promise is simple: record the exact version of every dependency so that installs are deterministic. In practice, determinism breaks in several documented ways.\n\nFirst, lockfiles are not portable across package manager versions. npm's `package-lock.json` has changed format multiple times (v1, v2, v3). A lockfile generated by npm 6 may not be fully understood by npm 7+, and the reverse is also true. Yarn's `yarn.lock` has similar versioning issues. If your CI uses a different Node.js image than your developers, the lockfile may be re-resolved, pulling in newer transitive dependencies.\n\nSecond, lockfiles do not capture platform-specific optional dependencies. A lockfile generated on Linux may omit optional dependencies that are required on macOS or Windows, or include ones that are not available. When the lockfile is used on a different platform, the package manager may fetch additional packages that were not locked.\n\nThird, lockfiles can be silently updated. Running `npm install` (as opposed to `npm ci`) will update the lockfile if the manifest has changed. Many CI pipelines use `npm install` out of habit, which defeats the purpose of having a lockfile at all.\n\nFourth, and most subtly, the lockfile only covers the dependency graph as resolved by the package manager. If your build process fetches dependencies from multiple sources (e.g., a private registry and a public one), the lockfile may not capture the full graph. Similarly, if you use peer dependencies or bundled dependencies, the lockfile may not reflect the actual runtime versions.\n\nThe counterintuitive part is that the lockfile is most reliable when you treat it as an output, not an input. You should generate it in a controlled environment and verify that it does not change between builds. If it does change, that is a signal that something in your dependency resolution is non-deterministic.\n\n## How dependency resolution actually works\n\nTo design a pinning strategy, you need a mental model of how package managers resolve versions. Most modern package managers (npm, Yarn, pip, Go modules, Cargo) use a variant of semantic versioning (semver) and a resolution algorithm that tries to find a single version that satisfies all constraints.\n\nThe process typically works like this:\n\n1. Read the manifest (e.g., `package.json`, `requirements.txt`, `go.mod`) for direct dependencies and their version constraints.\n2. For each dependency, fetch the list of available versions from the registry.\n3. Apply the constraints to select a version. If multiple constraints apply to the same package (because it is a transitive dependency of several direct dependencies), the resolver must find a version that satisfies all of them. If no such version exists, resolution fails.\n4. Recursively resolve the dependencies of the selected versions.\n5. Write the resolved versions to the lockfile.\n\nThe key point is that the resolver is not guaranteed to pick the latest version that satisfies the constraints. It picks a version according to the package manager's algorithm, which may prioritize the highest version, the lowest version, or the version that was already in the lockfile (if one exists). This is why the same manifest can produce different lockfiles on different machines or at different times.\n\nFor example, npm's resolver uses a \"first-past-the-post\" approach: it processes dependencies in the order they are declared, and for each dependency, it picks the highest version that satisfies the constraint. If two dependencies require different versions of the same package, npm may install both versions side by side (nested), which can lead to duplication and subtle bugs. Yarn's resolver uses a similar approach but with a different algorithm for deduplication.\n\nUnderstanding this mechanism matters because it tells you where pinning can help and where it cannot. Pinning a direct dependency to an exact version (`\"lodash\": \"4.17.21\"`) removes the resolver's freedom for that package. But it does not constrain transitive dependencies unless you also pin them, either by using a lockfile or by using a tool that can enforce a full dependency graph.\n\n## Pinning strategies: from loose to strict\n\nThere is a spectrum of pinning strategies, each with trade-offs. The right choice depends on your risk tolerance, team size, and deployment frequency.\n\n| Strategy | Manifest example | Lockfile | Pros | Cons |\n|----------|------------------|----------|------|------|\n| Range (default) | `\"lodash\": \"^4.17.0\"` | Yes | Easy to get security patches | Non-deterministic without lockfile; can pull breaking changes |\n| Exact direct pins | `\"lodash\": \"4.17.21\"` | Yes | Direct deps are stable | Transitive deps still float; manual updates |\n| Exact all pins (lockfile-only) | `\"lodash\": \"^4.17.0\"` + lockfile | Yes, committed | Deterministic if lockfile is respected | Lockfile can be bypassed; transitive deps not visible in manifest |\n| Full graph pinning | `\"lodash\": \"4.17.21\"` + overrides/resolutions | Yes, plus overrides | Complete control | High maintenance; can cause conflicts |\n| Vendoring | Copy source into repo | N/A | Ultimate reproducibility | Bloats repo; hard to patch |\n\n**Range pinning** is what you get by default. It is convenient because it allows patch and minor updates, but it is non-deterministic without a lockfile. Even with a lockfile, a fresh install (e.g., in a new environment) may resolve differently if the lockfile is not used or is outdated.\n\n**Exact direct pins** remove ambiguity for your direct dependencies. This is common in Python (`requirements.txt` with `==`) and Go (`go.mod` with exact versions). But it does not constrain transitive dependencies. If a direct dependency has a range for its own dependencies, those can still float.\n\n**Exact all pins** means you pin every dependency, direct and transitive, in the manifest. This is impractical for most teams because it requires manually tracking the entire graph. Some ecosystems support this via overrides (npm's `overrides` field, Yarn's `resolutions`, pip's constraints files). Overrides let you force a specific version of a transitive dependency, but they can cause conflicts if other packages require a different version.\n\n**Vendoring** means copying the source code of dependencies into your repository. This is common in Go (vendor directory) and some C/C++ projects. It gives you complete control and reproducibility, but it makes updates painful and bloats your repository.\n\nA pragmatic middle ground for backend teams: use exact pins for direct dependencies in the manifest, commit the lockfile, use `npm ci` or equivalent in CI to enforce the lockfile, and use overrides sparingly for known-vulnerable transitive dependencies. This gives you determinism for your direct dependencies and a record of the full graph, while still allowing security patches to be applied via lockfile updates.\n\n## Implementing a reproducible install in CI\n\nThe single most effective step you can take is to ensure that CI uses a command that fails if the lockfile is out of sync with the manifest. For npm, that is `npm ci`. For Yarn, `yarn install --frozen-lockfile`. For pip, `pip install --require-hashes` with a fully pinned requirements file. For Go, `go mod verify` and `go mod tidy` checks.\n\nHere is a minimal GitHub Actions workflow that enforces lockfile integrity for a Node.js project:\n\n```yaml\nname: CI\non: [push, pull_request]\njobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n        with:\n          node-version: '20'\n      - run: npm ci\n      - run: npm test\n```\n\nThe key line is `npm ci`. Unlike `npm install`, `npm ci` will fail if `package-lock.json` is missing or does not match `package.json`. It also installs from the lockfile without modifying it. This catches the common mistake of committing a manifest change without updating the lockfile.\n\nFor Python, a similar approach uses `pip-tools` to generate a fully pinned `requirements.txt` with hashes, and then installs with `pip install --require-hashes -r requirements.txt`. The `--require-hashes` flag ensures that every package is pinned to a specific version and hash, preventing dependency confusion attacks.\n\nFor Go, `go mod tidy` should be run in CI to ensure that `go.mod` and `go.sum` are consistent. The `go.sum` file contains hashes of module content, which provides integrity checking.\n\nBeyond the install command, you should also verify that the lockfile does not change during the build. A common pattern is to run the install, then check `git diff --exit-code` on the lockfile. If the lockfile changed, the build fails. This catches cases where the package manager silently updates the lockfile.\n\n## Measuring your dependency risk surface\n\nYou cannot manage what you do not measure. For dependency risk, the key metrics are:\n\n- **Number of direct dependencies**: A high count increases the attack surface and maintenance burden. There is no universal threshold, but teams often aim to keep direct dependencies in the dozens, not hundreds.\n- **Number of transitive dependencies**: This is usually much larger. Tools like `npm ls --all` or `pipdeptree` can show the full tree.\n- **Age of dependencies**: How long since each dependency was last updated? Stale dependencies are more likely to have unpatched vulnerabilities.\n- **Vulnerability count by severity**: SCA tools provide this, but raw counts are misleading. A better metric is the number of vulnerabilities that are reachable from your code (see next section).\n- **Time to remediate**: How long does it take from when a vulnerability is disclosed to when you deploy a fix? This is a leading indicator of process health.\n\nA practical way to measure these is to integrate an SCA tool into your CI pipeline and export the results to a dashboard. Many SCA tools (e.g., `npm audit`, `pip-audit`, `govulncheck`, OWASP Dependency-Check) can output JSON, which you can ingest into your observability stack. For example, `npm audit --json` produces a JSON report that you can parse and push to a metrics system.\n\nBut beware: raw vulnerability counts are noisy. A typical Node.js project with a few hundred transitive dependencies will have dozens of alerts, most of which are not exploitable in your context. The signal-to-noise ratio is poor unless you filter by reachability.\n\n## The failure modes that SCA tools miss\n\nSCA tools are good at matching package versions against vulnerability databases. They are bad at understanding whether a vulnerability is actually exploitable in your application. This gap leads to two failure modes: alert fatigue and false negatives.\n\n**Alert fatigue** happens when a tool reports hundreds of vulnerabilities, most of which are in development dependencies or in code paths you never execute. Teams learn to ignore the alerts, and then miss the one that matters. A common example is a vulnerability in a test framework or a build tool that is never deployed to production. Another is a vulnerability in a function that your code never calls.\n\n**False negatives** happen when a vulnerability exists but the SCA tool does not detect it. This can occur because the vulnerability is not yet in the database, because the tool does not support your package manager, or because the dependency is vendored or dynamically loaded. For example, a Python package that loads a native library at runtime may not be detected by a tool that only scans `requirements.txt`.\n\nTo mitigate these, you need to combine SCA with other techniques:\n\n- **Reachability analysis**: Some SCA tools can determine whether a vulnerable function is actually called by your code. This is not perfect, but it reduces noise significantly. For Go, `govulncheck` does this using call graph analysis.\n- **Runtime detection**: Tools like runtime application self-protection (RASP) or eBPF-based monitoring can detect exploitation attempts at runtime, catching vulnerabilities that SCA missed.\n- **Dependency review in pull requests**: Tools like GitHub's dependency review action can block PRs that introduce known-vulnerable dependencies, preventing new risk from entering the codebase.\n\nA typical failure mode is a team that runs SCA only on a nightly schedule, not on every PR. By the time the report is reviewed, the vulnerable dependency has already been merged and deployed. The fix is to shift SCA left: run it as a required check on every pull request, and fail the check if a new vulnerability of high severity is introduced.\n\n## When pinning is the wrong lever\n\nPinning is not a universal solution. There are cases where strict pinning causes more harm than good.\n\n**Rapidly evolving internal libraries**: If you have a shared internal library that changes frequently, pinning every service to an exact version creates a coordination nightmare. Every change requires updating every consumer. In this case, a range pin with a lockfile is better, combined with a policy that consumers update within a defined window.\n\n**Security patches**: If you pin to an exact version and do not have a process to update, you will miss security patches. Pinning without automation is a recipe for stale dependencies. You need a bot (e.g., Dependabot, Renovate) that opens PRs to update pinned versions, and you need the CI to verify that the update does not break anything.\n\n**Ecosystems with strong compatibility guarantees**: Go modules, for example, use minimal version selection (MVS) and semantic import versioning, which reduces the need for aggressive pinning. The Go team explicitly recommends against pinning to exact versions in `go.mod` for libraries, because it can cause version conflicts for consumers. Instead, Go's tooling handles reproducibility via `go.sum` and the module graph.\n\n**Monorepos with many packages**: In a monorepo, pinning every package to exact versions can lead to a large number of duplicate versions in the dependency graph, increasing build times and bundle sizes. Tools like pnpm and Yarn PnP have different strategies for handling this, but strict pinning may not be the best fit.\n\nThe key is to match your pinning strategy to your risk profile and your team's ability to maintain it. A small team with a few services can afford exact pins and manual updates. A large organization with hundreds of services needs automation and a more nuanced policy.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**Should I commit the lockfile?**\nYes, for applications. The lockfile is what makes installs reproducible across machines and CI. For libraries that are published to a registry, the lockfile is typically not published, and consumers will resolve their own versions. In that case, you should test against a range of dependency versions to ensure compatibility.\n\n**How often should I update dependencies?**\nThere is no single answer, but a common policy is to apply security patches within days, minor updates within weeks, and major updates on a planned cadence. The important thing is to have an automated process (e.g., Renovate or Dependabot) that opens PRs, and a CI pipeline that catches regressions. Manual updates tend to be deferred indefinitely.\n\n**What is the difference between SCA and dependency pinning?**\nSCA is about identifying known vulnerabilities in your dependencies. Pinning is about controlling which versions you use. They are complementary: pinning without SCA leaves you exposed to known vulnerabilities; SCA without pinning leaves you with a moving target that is hard to audit. A mature process uses both.\n\n**Why does my lockfile change when I run npm install?**\nBecause `npm install` updates the lockfile to match the manifest and the latest available versions that satisfy the constraints. If you want to install exactly what is in the lockfile without modifying it, use `npm ci`. If the lockfile changes unexpectedly, it may be because the manifest was modified, or because the registry state changed (e.g., a new version was published that satisfies a range).\n\n## Start by auditing your lockfile\n\nThe next concrete step is to check whether your CI actually enforces the lockfile. Open your CI configuration file (e.g., `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `.gitlab-ci.yml`, `Jenkinsfile`) and search for the install command. If it is `npm install`, `yarn install` without `--frozen-lockfile`, or `pip install -r requirements.txt` without hashes, you have a gap. Replace it with `npm ci`, `yarn install --frozen-lockfile`, or `pip install --require-hashes`, commit the change, and observe whether the build still passes. If it fails, you have just discovered that your lockfile was out of sync — which means your builds were not reproducible. Fixing that is the highest-leverage dependency management change you can make today.\n\n## Official documentation\n\n- [ebpf.io: What is eBPF?](https://ebpf.io/what-is-ebpf/)\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
